<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">White Croats</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 992 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A shadowy Slavic people north of the Carpathians, named by a Byzantine emperor as the homeland of the Croats of the Adriatic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> wrote in the 10th century of a White or unbaptised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, from which some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> had migrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in the 7th century. Historians usually place the White Croats on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, on both slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, but their exact territory is disputed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Very little is known of their society. They appear to have lived in fortified hill settlements, such as the large site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilsko">Stilsko</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, and to have farmed and herded in the Carpathian foothills. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> lists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavic</a> tribes who took part in Oleg's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 907.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 992 Vladimir I of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, and they disappear from the sources soon afterwards. Their eastern lands became part of Rus' and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Principality of Galicia</a>, while the western parts around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-poland">Duchy of Poland</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/white-croats.jpg" alt="White Croats" width="320" height="398"><figcaption>Image: SeikoEn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Slavic_tribes_peoples_8th_9th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 992 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Early Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carpathian Croats</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=796&amp;focus=white-croats" aria-label="White Croats on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5402 437 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where White Croats lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5722 693h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5722 693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ukraine. <a href="/atlas?year=796&amp;focus=white-croats">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

